From 205993443df6d7c8b2cf914a70b7433d4c289ff2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 07:45:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] unloaded sweep re-renders a partly delivered claim
 range

The local unloaded sweeper claims a subscription's whole cursor range and wrote one
notice keyed by every formatted event in it. Since #1164 the writer refuses a row whose
kanban_event_keys only partly overlap the chat's lineage (KanbanNoticeOverlapError).
When a live poller had written the row for an earlier event (e.g. blocked) and died
before its ack, every sweep pass on the passive-row path raised, rewound the cursor and
logged a traceback, and the later done notice waited until the chat was opened.

_deliver_sub now drops claimed events whose '<board>:<event id>' key is already in the
tip's lineage (kanban_notice_keys_delivered) before rendering text and metadata; a range
with nothing left is acked without a row. A failed key read keeps the whole range, and
the writer's atomic recheck still guards a race after the filter: it raises, rewinds and
the next pass re-filters.

SweepBatches.flush also catches OverflowError (datetime.fromtimestamp of an absurd owner
time such as -1e30), as collect already does, so one corrupt record releases only its
own chat's claims instead of aborting the flush for every other chat.

Proof: the four tests in test_kanban_unloaded_partial_overlap.py and
test_absurd_owner_time_in_one_chat_never_holds_another_chats_group fail on main
(6d9af3bb7d) and pass with the fix; the except-clause mutant is killed by the
failed-key-read test. 22 kanban unloaded/remote/at-least-once files: 260 passed.

Card: t_01a1ce5a5b6f9bb3
